(Title) Tj
T*
(Feasibility and Efficacy of Wearable Robotic Assistance for Motor Rehabilitation in) Tj
T*
(Parkinson Disease: A Pilot Randomized Controlled Study Protocol) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Motor dysfunction and gait impairment in Parkinson disease significantly restrict mobility) Tj
T*
(and independence. Emerging robotic technologies may augment traditional physical) Tj
T*
(rehabilitation by providing consistent, quantifiable assistance. This pilot protocol) Tj
T*
(evaluates whether supplementing conventional physical therapy with structured sessions in) Tj
T*
(a soft robotic exoskeleton improves locomotor function and motor symptoms in patients with) Tj
T*
(Parkinson disease. Approximately 56 individuals with Parkinson disease will be randomly) Tj
T*
(allocated to either conventional rehabilitation \(control\) or identical conventional) Tj
T*
(therapy augmented with soft exoskeleton-assisted training. All participants will receive) Tj
T*
(five sessions weekly for four weeks. Primary outcomes include gait velocity and stride) Tj
T*
(symmetry. Secondary measures encompass ankle range of motion, motor severity scales, and) Tj
T*
(cognitive assessment. We hypothesize that robotic assistance during training facilitates) Tj
T*
(greater locomotor gains than conventional therapy alone.) Tj
ET
